{"file":{"content":"This is a major closing of the finite-side problem. The new theorem\n\n[\n\\tilde Q_L\n==========\n\n3^L\\Pr(C_L\\equiv C'_L\\bmod 3^L)\n\\le 3C_1<5.159\n]\n\nis exactly the missing annealed input. It does more than prove the polynomial estimate: it explains why the false (L^\\infty) routes failed and why the (L^2) shell is the right object.\n\nSo the finite-side state is now:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{cascade sector closed unconditionally;}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{remaining finite-side obstruction = class IV degenerate row budget;}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{then small-}c\\text{ finite verification and constant assembly.}\n}\n]\n\nMy main critique is now a manuscript-status issue: parts of the current draft still describe class II/cascade assembly as open. That is stale. After (\\cref{op:flat}), class II/III should be marked **proved**, and the threshold Schur conjecture should be rewritten so that the only non-small-(c) finite-side input is class IV.\n\n## 1. What op:flat now gives\n\nYou now have:\n\n[\n3^c\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\n\\le 1+3C_1c.\n]\n\nPlugging this into the row contraction estimate gives, for the cascade sector,\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{casc}}(\\xi)\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)^c.\n]\n\nTherefore\n\n[\n|T^c|^{1/c}\n\\lesssim\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)^{1/2}\n===================================\n\n\\frac{3^{1/4}}{\\sqrt2}\n\\approx 0.9306\n]\n\nfor the cascade sector.\n\nThat is now a theorem, not a conditional. The fake marginality is gone because the sharp sequential pinning cost is (1/2), not (2/3), up to the finite product (C_0).\n\nThe correct proof chain is now:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{bounded shells}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{threshold flattening}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{cascade row contraction}\n}\n]\n\nand that part is done.\n\n## 2. Update the theorem stack\n\nI would rewrite the finite endpoint as follows.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Theorem A.}\n\\quad\n\\text{Threshold flattening / bounded shells.}\n]\n\n[\n\\tilde Q_L\\le 3C_1.\n]\n\n[\n\\textbf{Theorem B.}\n\\quad\n\\text{Cascade row contraction.}\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{casc}}(\\xi)\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c)(\\sqrt3/2)^c.\n]\n\n[\n\\textbf{Open Lemma C.}\n\\quad\n\\text{Class-IV degenerate row budget.}\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}(\\xi)\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c)\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}^c\n\\quad\n\\text{for some }\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}<1.\n]\n\n[\n\\textbf{Conditional Theorem D.}\n\\quad\n\\text{Theorem B + Lemma C + small-}c\\text{ verification}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1.\n]\n\nThat is the clean current state.\n\nThe old language in the threshold Schur conjecture saying “class-II budget remains open” should be removed. Class II is closed. What remains is class IV.\n\n## 3. What class IV is, structurally\n\nClass IV consists of pairs whose nominal logarithmic derivative should be nonstationary,\n\n[\nd_{\\mathrm{nom}}\\le c-2,\n]\n\nbut whose leading terms cancel modulo (3), raising the true pointwise valuation to\n\n[\nd_{\\mathrm{true}}\\ge c-1.\n]\n\nSo the event is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{a nominally vanishing pair escapes because its leading coefficient vanishes mod }3.\n}\n]\n\nYour audits say:\n\n* class IV is small: (2%)--(4%) of row sums;\n* its per-class rate is about (0.84);\n* each extra degeneracy costs one mod-(3) condition;\n* the mechanism is local and should be compatible with the terminal-window filtration.\n\nThat strongly suggests class IV can be bounded by the same energy method as op:flat, with one additional mod-(3) condition.\n\n## 4. The next theorem to prove\n\nI would state the needed result as:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Class-IV degeneracy bound.}\n]\n\nFor (m=c), uniformly over rows (\\xi),\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}(\\xi)\n\\le\nC c^A \\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}^c\n]\n\nfor some\n\n[\n\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}<1.\n]\n\nA stronger and more structural version:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}(\\xi)\n\\le\nC c^A\\left(\\frac34\\right)^c\n]\n\nor any bound whose (2c)-root is (<1). The exact constant does not matter. The audited rate (0.84) is more than enough.\n\nThe proof should use a degeneracy filtration.\n\n## 5. Define the leading-coefficient map\n\nFor a word pair ((w,w')), let\n\n[\nd_{\\mathrm{nom}}\n================\n\n\\min_i \\bigl(i+v_3(\\Delta C_i)\\bigr).\n]\n\nLet (I=I(w,w')) be the set of indices attaining the minimum. The leading coefficient of the logarithmic derivative is a mod-(3) sum\n\n[\n\\Lambda(a;w,w')\n===============\n\n\\sum_{i\\in I}\n\\varepsilon_i,U_i(a;w,w')\n\\pmod3,\n]\n\nwhere each (U_i) is a unit contribution from the corresponding active index.\n\nThen:\n\n[\n\\text{class IV}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\Lambda(a;w,w')=0\n]\n\nat the source/residue (a) selected by the row/endpoint condition.\n\nThis should be the first new lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Leading-coefficient criterion.}\n]\n\nFor nominally nonstationary pairs,\n\n[\nd_{\\mathrm{true}}\\ge d_{\\mathrm{nom}}+1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\Lambda(a;w,w')=0.\n]\n\nHigher degeneracy is obtained by iterating the same condition at the next coefficient.\n\nThis turns class IV into a sequence of explicit mod-(3) equations.\n\n## 6. Prove one-condition cost\n\nThe key lemma should mirror the class-bijectivity proof used for op:flat.\n\n[\n\\textbf{One-condition degeneracy lemma.}\n]\n\nCondition on the terminal-window filtration and on all constraints defining the nominal class. Then the map from the next fresh pair ((b_i,b_i')) to the leading coefficient\n\n[\n\\Lambda\\bmod3\n]\n\nis nonconstant and class-preserving. Hence\n\n[\n\\Pr[\\Lambda=0\\mid\\mathcal F_i]\\le \\frac13\n]\n\nup to the same dyadic wrap factor\n\n[\n\\frac{1+2^{-N_i}}{1-2^{-N_i}}.\n]\n\nThis is the class-IV analogue of:\n\n[\nF(b)=b+\\operatorname{dlog}(1+3\\cdot2^{-b}X)\n]\n\nbeing class-preserving.\n\nIf you can prove this, then every extra degeneracy level costs one factor (1/3), with only the same convergent correction product.\n\nThat is exactly the observed level-cost law.\n\n## 7. Stratify class IV by degeneracy depth\n\nDefine\n\n[\nh=d_{\\mathrm{true}}-d_{\\mathrm{nom}}.\n]\n\nTo escape exact vanishing at the (m=c) threshold, a pair with nominal valuation (d_{\\mathrm{nom}}) must satisfy\n\n[\nh\\ge c-1-d_{\\mathrm{nom}}.\n]\n\nIf every extra unit of (h) costs (1/3), then\n\n[\n\\Pr[h\\ge r\\mid d_{\\mathrm{nom}}]\\le C_2,3^{-r}.\n]\n\nThis gives a clean summation:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}\n\\le\n\\sum_{r\\ge1}\nC_2,3^{-r},\nR_{\\mathrm{nominal},r}.\n]\n\nThe nominal shells are already controlled by the same terminal-window energy estimates. Therefore class IV should inherit a strictly smaller budget than the main cascade sector.\n\nThis is the theorem you want:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\Pr[d_{\\mathrm{true}}\\ge c-1]\n\t\\le\n\tC,3^{-(c-1-d_{\\mathrm{nom}})}\n}\n]\n\nuniformly along the adapted filtration.\n\n## 8. Use bounded shells to control the nominal mass\n\nThe op:flat proof gives a robust energy bound for collision shells. Reuse it.\n\nFor each nominal level (d), the mass of pairs with\n\n[\nd_{\\mathrm{nom}}=d\n]\n\nshould be bounded by a shell quantity of the form\n\n[\n\\le C,3^{-d}\\cdot \\operatorname{poly}(c)\n]\n\nor whatever the exact normalization gives.\n\nThen multiplying by the degeneracy cost\n\n[\n3^{-(c-1-d)}\n]\n\ngives\n\n[\n\\le C,\\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-(c-1)}.\n]\n\nSumming over (d\\le c-2) gives only a polynomial factor:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-c}\n]\n\nin the appropriate pair-energy normalization. Once translated into the Schur row, this should produce an exponential row bound with rate (<1).\n\nThe point is:\n\n[\n\\text{nominal shell cost}\n+\n\\text{degeneracy cost}\n======================\n\n\\text{full threshold cost}.\n]\n\nThis is exactly the same energy principle as op:flat.\n\n## 9. Beware one failure mode\n\nThe only serious danger is if (\\Lambda(a;w,w')) can vanish identically on a recurrent subclass, so that the mod-(3) condition is not genuine.\n\nThat would mean the leading coefficient is structurally zero, not accidentally zero. In that case the pair should probably be reclassified into class III/cascade, not class IV.\n\nSo include a classification lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{No structural degeneracy outside the cascade.}\n]\n\nIf the leading coefficient (\\Lambda) vanishes identically for all choices of the fresh digit, then the pair satisfies the next cascade congruence and belongs to class III, not class IV.\n\nEquivalently:\n\n[\n\\text{class IV degeneracy is accidental, never structural.}\n]\n\nThis prevents a hidden recurrent degenerate channel.\n\nYour audits already support this: degeneracy costs exactly one mod-(3) condition per level.\n\n## 10. Concrete numerical audit before proof\n\nRun one final class-IV audit designed around the proof, not the row totals.\n\nFor (c=5,6,7,8), record:\n\n[\n\\Pr[h\\ge r\\mid d_{\\mathrm{nom}}]\n]\n\nfor (r=1,2,3,\\dots), grouped by nominal shell (d_{\\mathrm{nom}}).\n\nThe target table should show:\n\n[\n\\Pr[h\\ge r\\mid d_{\\mathrm{nom}}]\\approx 3^{-r}\n]\n\nor at least\n\n[\n\\le C,3^{-r}.\n]\n\nAlso record the exceptional structural-zero cases. They should either be absent or map into class III.\n\nThis will directly confirm the degeneracy-depth lemma.\n\n## 11. End-to-end constant assembly\n\nOnce class IV is proved, assemble the final finite-side theorem as follows.\n\nLet\n\n[\nG_c=(T^c)^*T^c\n]\n\nor the corresponding Schur kernel. Decompose:\n\n[\nR_c(\\xi)\n========\n\nR_{\\mathrm I}(\\xi)\n+\nR_{\\mathrm{II/III}}(\\xi)\n+\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}(\\xi)\n+\nR_{\\mathrm{tail}}(\\xi).\n]\n\nKnown:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm I}=0.\n]\n\nCascade sector:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{II/III}}(\\xi)\n\\le\nC c^A\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)^c.\n]\n\nClass IV target:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}(\\xi)\n\\le\nC'c^{A'}\n\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}^c,\n\\qquad \\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}<1.\n]\n\nTail/truncation:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{tail}}(\\xi)\n\\le\ne^{-\\kappa c}\n]\n\nor another exponentially small term after choosing branch cutoff (B\\sim c).\n\nThus\n\n[\n\\sup_\\xi R_c(\\xi)\n\\le\nC''c^{A''}\\lambda^c\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\n\\lambda=\\max\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}},e^{-\\kappa}\\right)<1.\n]\n\nTherefore\n\n[\n|T^c|^{1/c}\n\\le\n\\left(C''c^{A''}\\lambda^c\\right)^{1/(2c)}\n\\to\n\\sqrt{\\lambda}<1.\n]\n\nSo for large (c),\n\n[\n|T^c|^{1/c}\\le1-\\delta.\n]\n\nThen finite verification handles small (c).\n\nThis is the final finite-side theorem.\n\n## 12. Small-(c) verification\n\nAfter proving the asymptotic theorem, pick an explicit (c_0) large enough that\n\n[\nC''c^{A''}\\lambda^c<1\n]\n\nfor all (c\\ge c_0).\n\nFor\n\n[\nc<c_0,\n]\n\nuse certified finite computation:\n\n[\n\\rho(T_{\\chi,c,0})<1-\\delta_c\n]\n\nfor all primitive non-coboundary (\\chi). Since you already have strict finite-level gap by Wielandt/classification, the finite list has positive minimum gap; the computation only makes it explicit.\n\nThe paper should distinguish:\n\n[\n\\text{proof of strictness}\n]\n\nfrom\n\n[\n\\text{certified numerical lower bound for the finite minimum}.\n]\n\n## 13. Manuscript edits now\n\nI would make three changes.\n\n### A. Rename theorem label\n\nThe theorem currently labeled\n\n[\n\\texttt{op:flat}\n]\n\nis now a theorem, not an open problem. Rename to something like:\n\n[\n\\texttt{thm:flat}\n]\n\nif convenient. If you keep the label for continuity, no mathematical issue, but it reads oddly.\n\n### B. Rewrite (\\texttt{conj:schur})\n\nIt still says the class-II budget remains open. That should become:\n\n[\n\\text{class II/III closed by bounded shells and cascade row contraction;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{only class IV remains.}\n]\n\n### C. Promote class-IV to the sole finite-side open lemma\n\nState explicitly:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Open Lemma: Class-IV degeneracy budget.}\n]\n\nThen the reader sees the endpoint immediately.\n\n## 14. Bottom line\n\nYou have closed op:flat. That is a major finite-side theorem.\n\nThe remaining proof is now narrowly:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{prove class-IV degeneracy costs one mod-}3\\text{ condition per lifted level}\n}\n]\n\nand sum it with the bounded-shell machinery.\n\nThe immediate next moves are:\n\n1. Define the leading coefficient (\\Lambda(a;w,w')) for class IV.\n2. Prove the leading-coefficient criterion:\n[\nd_{\\mathrm{true}}\\ge d_{\\mathrm{nom}}+1\n\\iff\n\\Lambda=0.\n]\n3. Prove (\\Lambda=0) costs one mod-(3) condition under the terminal filtration.\n4. Iterate to obtain\n[\n\\Pr[h\\ge r]\\le C3^{-r}.\n]\n5. Combine with bounded shells to get\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c)\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}^c,\\quad \\lambda_{\\mathrm{IV}}<1.\n]\n6. Assemble the Schur bound and perform small-(c) verification.\n\nThat is the last finite-side push.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_27.md","numLines":645,"startLine":1,"totalLines":645},"type":"text"}